1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,940
Pennies to the Moon would cost two and a half billion dollars.

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:06,580
Sounds random, so let's build it slowly.

3
00:00:07,280 --> 00:00:10,440
A single penny is only about one and a half millimeters thick.

4
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:14,540
The Moon is about two hundred thirty-nine thousand miles away.

5
00:00:15,080 --> 00:00:17,780
Pretend the stack is perfectly straight and never falls.

6
00:00:18,400 --> 00:00:22,620
So how many pennies do you need? About two hundred fifty-three billion.

7
00:00:23,100 --> 00:00:24,620
Hit like if you'd lose count around twelve.

8
00:00:25,540 --> 00:00:27,020
Now, a hundred pennies make one dollar.

9
00:00:27,540 --> 00:00:30,980
So the whole stack costs about two point five three billion dollars.

10
00:00:31,800 --> 00:00:35,000
The biggest lottery jackpot ever was about two billion dollars.

11
00:00:35,440 --> 00:00:38,820
So one record jackpot gets your tower most of the way there.

12
00:00:39,340 --> 00:00:41,740
Send this to the friend with a jar of pennies on their dresser.

13
00:00:42,200 --> 00:00:45,080
Now here's the twist. Swap the pennies for dollar bills.

14
00:00:45,840 --> 00:00:48,400
Bills are way thinner, so you need way more of them.

15
00:00:48,740 --> 00:00:52,520
A stack of dollar bills to the Moon would cost about thirty-five trillion dollars.

16
00:00:53,360 --> 00:00:55,260
Follow if your jaw just dropped a little.

17
00:00:55,260 --> 00:00:58,700
That's around seventeen thousand record lottery jackpots.

18
00:00:59,280 --> 00:01:02,320
So the cheapest ladder to the Moon is the coin everyone ignores.

19
00:01:02,920 --> 00:01:06,940
Split the penny tower across everyone on Earth, and it's thirty-one cents each.

20
00:01:07,480 --> 00:01:10,060
Would you chip in your thirty-one cents? Yes or no.

